1.410.e:
“A Buddha’s risen in the world,
an Eyeful One named Sumedha;
he’s helping all the folk to cross;
he will ferry you across too.”

The three fires are blown out in me;
all new existence is destroyed;
knowing well all the defilements,
I am now living, undefiled.

1.414.e:
This is the final time for me;
my last rebirth is proceeding;
today umbrella-carrying
is happening all of the time.

1.420.e:
What should be done has all been done;
I have put an end to rebirths.
All defilements are exhausted;
now there will be no more rebirth.

1.425.e:
There’s nothing for me to give you;
I live eating only wild fruits.
But I do have this seat to give;
sit down on this couch made of sticks.”

1.429.e:
“In flux indeed is all that is;
things that arise and then decline
are being born and then dying;
happy is the relief from them.”

1.440.e:
This is the final time for me;
my last rebirth is proceeding.
The three knowledges are attained;
I have done what the Buddha taught!

1.448.e:
My defilements are now burnt up;
all new existence is destroyed.
All defilements are exhausted;
I am living without constraint.

1.454.e:
The three fires are blown out in me;
all new existence is destroyed;
all the defilements are exhausted,
I am now living, undefiled.

1.472.e:
I answered them, “Don’t be frightened;
there is nothing for you to fear.
Let all of you be confident;
this omen portends happiness.

1.486.e:
The three fires are blown out in me;
all new existence is destroyed;
knowing well all the defilements,
I am now dwelling undefiled.

1.489.e:
“The earth, bull-like, is bellowing;
it’s roaring like the king of beasts.
It’s shaking like a crocodile;
what will this be the result of?”

1.491.e:
The one named Māyā’s going to be
the birth-mother of this person;
Suddhodhana the father’s name;
this one will be named Gotama.

A long time, now come together with you;
not destroyed, again the interval was long;
today, again come together with you,
O Gotama, deeds do not just disappear.

1.500.e:
I do not have an advisor;
there is no one who speaks to me;
no teacher and no preceptor,
I come to a home in the woods.

Due to worshipping the stupa,
I am not soiled with dirt and dust;
my limbs are not exuding sweat;
I’m radiantly beautiful.

1.530.e:
And it has another virtue,
being reborn life after life;
for it there are no enemies:
the result of thrown-away leaves.

1.532.e:
This is Buddha, the Blessed One;
he’s the Unsurpassed, the Lion,
the Turner of the Brahma-wheel
for the world including the gods;

All my defilements are destroyed;
I’ve crossed the ocean of being.
Like fire, I have no attachments;
I will realize nirvana.

1.540.e:
My brother lived near the river;
he was named “Nadī Kassapa.”
The other lived close to Gāyā;
by name he’s “Gāyā Kassapa”.

1.541.e:
“I’m not getting ordained, Hero,
in your well-preached dispensation;
thus I’m miserable with grief;
be my refuge, O Leader.”

1.542.e:
Merit and evil are all destroyed;
every illness is driven out.
Like fire, I have no attachments;
I will realize nirvana.

1.544.e:
I had lots of doubts, here and there,
what is proper, what’s improper;
while preaching the supreme Dhamma,
the Buddha resolved all of that.
